From 87ebbef74c06c2b259d48ee31949b7e447943e7b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lccc-agent <agent@lccc.local>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 17:01:14 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/5] i686: store constant call arguments directly instead of
 routing them through %eax

`emit_call_stack_args` marshalled EVERY stack argument through the
accumulator:

    self.operand_to_eax(&args[i]);                        // movl $imm, %eax   (or xorl %eax,%eax)
    emit!(self.state, "    movl %eax, {}(%esp)", off);    // movl %eax, N(%esp)

For an operand that is already a constant that is two instructions and a
gratuitous `%eax` clobber where one suffices.  gcc, clang and icx all pass the
constant directly (`pushl $.LC1`).  This was the dominant term in the oracle
comparison that motivated the work: on `narrow_shift_count_ge_width.c`
(`-O2 -m32 -fno-pic`, function `main`) lccc emitted 403 instructions against
gcc 291 / clang 295 / icx 300, with 236 `movl` against gcc's 22, and 37% of its
instructions carrying a memory operand against gcc's 1%.

Root cause rather than symptom: the fix is at the emission site, not a peephole
that cleans up afterwards.  A peephole would have to prove the materializing
register is dead, which caps the win; at the lowering site the constant is
never materialized at all, so the `%eax` clobber disappears too and the
downstream reloads it caused disappear with it.  That is why the measured win
is 2.3x the census of provably-dead pairs.

Only the CONSTANT path is bypassed.  `%eax` is still required for a value in a
slot (x86 has no memory-to-memory move, and for an alloca what is passed is the
slot's ADDRESS, a `leal` or an over-alignment `leal`/`addl`/`andl` triple) and
for the legacy no-home acc-flow case where the value is only implicitly in
`%eax`.  The accumulator cache is deliberately left alone: `%eax` is not
written, so an entry claiming it holds a value stays true, where the old path
had to invalidate it.

Encoding arithmetic, not a heuristic, decides which constants fold.  Zero is
excluded because it is the one class that grows:

    non-zero   movl $imm,%eax (5B) + movl %eax,d8(%esp) (4B) = 9B  ->  movl $imm,d8(%esp) = 8B
    zero       xorl %eax,%eax (2B) + movl %eax,d8(%esp) (4B) = 6B  ->  movl $0,d8(%esp)   = 8B

Folding zeros too was measured: -1,194 instructions but +1,344 .text bytes
(+0.477%), with 327 of the 432 census sites being zeros.  Restricting to
non-zero keeps 83% of the instruction win and 57% less byte growth.  Runtime
was neutral either way, so there is no measured performance argument for
paying bytes.

Evidence (i686, 809-TU tests/ corpus, fastbuild, `CCC_NO_CONST_STACK_ARG` gate):

  origin/main                          270,945 insns / 102,007 slot refs
  + CFG slot forwarding (parent)       270,698 / 101,602
  + this change                        269,705 / 101,601
  this change alone:                     -993 insns, 298 TUs smaller, 0 larger
  .text on a 210-TU sample:              +580 bytes (+0.206%), 35 TUs smaller,
                                         20 larger; +638 of the +580 is two
                                         memset TUs (22,573 -> 22,892 each),
                                         i.e. everything else nets smaller.
                                         The growth is downstream
                                         scratch-register reallocation, not the
                                         fold itself: `movl %ebx, %eax` copies
                                         appear where the removed `%eax` clobber
                                         used to supply a scratch definition.
  gate neutrality: with the gate set, the object file is BYTE-IDENTICAL to the
                                         parent build (sha256 220b4c89... on
                                         cpu_model_memset_inline.c), and the
                                         corpus counts reproduce exactly.
  runtime: lz4_compress and 12 further binding benchmarks hash-IDENTICAL, so
                                         the no-regression constraint holds by
                                         construction; paired alternating timing
                                         of the wider (zero-inclusive) variant
                                         was geomean -0.24% / median +0.13%.
  correctness: cargo test 2,606 passed / 0 failed; fmt and clippy clean;
                                         differential fuzzer 240/240 agree with
                                         gcc and with the gated arm (37 fired);
                                         execution differential over 666 TUs
                                         that build and run under the gcc
                                         oracle: 21 mismatches, exactly the 21
                                         the parent build also has -- no new
                                         miscompiles.

Known follow-ups this exposes, both measured rather than guessed:

  * `emit_call_8byte_stack_arg` copies a slot to the outgoing area through
    `%eax` in four instructions (`movl sr0,%eax; movl %eax,N(%esp); movl
    sr4,%eax; movl %eax,N+4(%esp)`).  x87 `fldl`/`fstpl` would be two but is
    not bit-exact for signalling-NaN payloads, and SSE `movsd` would be two and
    exact but needs a target-feature gate; neither belongs in this change.
  * The real size lever is the outgoing-argument strategy itself: lccc
    pre-allocates with `subl $N,%esp` and stores through `disp(%esp)` at 4-11
    bytes per argument, where gcc pushes at 1-6 (`pushl $0` is 2 bytes, `pushl
    %reg` is 1).  On a 7-argument printf that is ~13 bytes for gcc against
    34-62 for lccc.  Converting is a calling-sequence change touching
    esp_adjust bookkeeping, CFI, alignment, varargs, struct-byval and the
    `%esp` renumbering in the CFG slot forwarder, so it needs its own A/B.
