From 699cec9742b48188768592366f2dc028622e96a3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: amcheck <am@check>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 17:48:42 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 5/5] docs(i686): record the per-arm predicate that was
 measured and reverted, and the new tests

* New section 6.4: the emission-time byte model is not the delivered byte model.
  The i686 peephole normalizes 'movl $0,%reg' to 'xorl %reg,%reg', so a zero
  argument costs 2B in every IrConst arm and the folded 8B store loses in all of
  them. The per-arm predicate that the emission-time model implied was
  implemented, unit-tested and measured; a per-TU assembly diff against the
  already-validated binary found 8 TUs each growing 2 bytes, and it was reverted
  before commit. The census that appeared to license it (0 'movl $0' sites
  against 562 'xorl' sites) was counting final assembly, where the form no
  longer exists: the data was right and the inference was not.
* Red-team item 11 generalizes it: a cost model must be evaluated against final
  output, and a test derived from the same model as the code cannot falsify it --
  only an independent differential measurement can. That is what caught this.
* Section 5.4 records the 6 new unit tests (lib total 2,618) and the new
  end-to-end regression test with its pass matrix and its fire counts, so the
  test is demonstrably not vacuous.
* Section 9 adds zero_arm_census.py and asm_identity.py, the latter being the
  harness that proves a compiler refactor output-neutral (795/795 TUs identical
  at five flag sets) rather than merely believed neutral.
* Header updated for the six-file deliverable.
